# Endocarditis, Myocarditis & Pericarditis (ID lecture) — 18 image-based items
Q = [
dict(img='janeway', cap='Plantar surface of the right foot',
stem="A 56-year-old man receiving hemodialysis through a tunneled central venous catheter is brought to the emergency department because of fever, shaking chills, and increasing shortness of breath for 5 days. He started no new medications in the past 3 months. His temperature is 39.6°C (103.3°F), pulse is 118/min, and blood pressure is 128/54 mm Hg. A decrescendo diastolic murmur is heard along the left sternal border. The lesions shown on the sole of his foot are flat, nonblanching, and <b>not</b> tender. There is no generalized rash. Platelet count is 214,000/mm<sup>3</sup>. Two sets of blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in clusters.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely mechanism of the skin lesions?",
choices=["Capillary bleeding from a low platelet count","Deposition of immune complexes in dermal vessels","Drug-induced hypersensitivity vasculitis","Septic emboli forming small dermal abscesses","Superantigen-driven capillary leak and rash"], key=3,
exp="Painless, flat, hemorrhagic macules on the sole in a patient with bacteremia and a new aortic regurgitation murmur are <b>Janeway lesions</b> of infective endocarditis. Here it is probably <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i> from a dialysis catheter.<br><br>A friable vegetation → fragments break off → <b>septic microemboli</b> travel through the arteries → lodge in dermal vessels → small abscesses with hemorrhage → painless macules on the palms and soles. The embolic group also includes splinter hemorrhages, petechiae and organ infarcts. The immune-complex group (Osler nodes, Roth spots, glomerulonephritis, positive rheumatoid factor) works by a different mechanism. Janeway lesions are a vascular <b>minor</b> Duke criterion.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Janeway lesions are painless macules on the palms and soles caused by septic microemboli. Osler nodes are painful and caused by immune complexes.",
wrong={0:"Thrombocytopenia causes widespread petechiae and bruising, but his platelet count is normal (214,000/mm<sup>3</sup>).",
1:"Immune-complex deposition causes <b>Osler nodes</b>: tender, raised nodules on the finger and toe pads. These lesions are flat and painless (\"Osler = Ouch\").",
2:"Drug hypersensitivity vasculitis causes palpable purpura, mostly on the legs, within weeks of a new drug. He has started no new medications.",
4:"Superantigen-driven toxic shock gives a diffuse sunburn-like rash, later desquamation, and hypotension. His rash is localized and his blood pressure is maintained."},
eli5="Tiny infected crumbs broke off the germ-covered clump on his heart valve and got stuck in the small blood vessels of his soles. Each one made a little painless red-purple spot. That tells you the spots came from bits flying off the valve."),

dict(img='roth', cap='Funduscopic examination of the left eye',
stem="A 58-year-old man with a known bicuspid aortic valve comes to the office because of low-grade fevers, night sweats, fatigue, and a 6-kg (13-lb) weight loss over 7 weeks. He had a dental extraction 2 months ago. His temperature is 38.1°C (100.6°F). An early diastolic murmur is heard at the left sternal border. The spleen tip is palpable. Funduscopic examination is shown.{FIG}The retinal finding in this patient is caused by the same mechanism as which of the following?",
choices=["Focal glomerulonephritis with hematuria","Linear hemorrhages beneath the fingernails","Mycotic aneurysm of a cerebral artery","Painless hemorrhagic macules on the soles","Wedge-shaped infarct of the spleen"], key=0,
exp="The arrows point to <b>Roth spots</b>: retinal hemorrhages with pale centres. This man has <b>subacute infective endocarditis</b>: a damaged (bicuspid) valve, a recent dental extraction (viridans streptococci), weeks of low-grade fever and weight loss, and splenomegaly.<br><br>A chronic infection → sustained antibody production → <b>circulating immune complexes</b> → deposited in small vessels → Roth spots (retina), <b>Osler nodes</b> (finger pads), <b>glomerulonephritis</b> (hematuria, red cell casts), and a positive rheumatoid factor. All four are immunologic minor Duke criteria.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Roth spots, Osler nodes, glomerulonephritis and positive rheumatoid factor are immune-complex phenomena of infective endocarditis. Janeway lesions, splinter hemorrhages, petechiae and infarcts are embolic.",
wrong={1:"Splinter hemorrhages come from capillary hemorrhage and microemboli, not immune complexes.",
2:"A mycotic aneurysm forms when a septic embolus lodges in the wall of an artery and infects it. It is an embolic, vascular phenomenon.",
3:"Painless hemorrhagic macules on the soles are Janeway lesions, which are septic microemboli forming microabscesses.",
4:"A wedge-shaped splenic infarct is caused by a fragment of vegetation blocking a splenic artery branch, which is embolic."},
eli5="A long-lasting infection makes the body pump out lots of antibodies. They clump with germ pieces into sticky lumps that jam in tiny vessels of the eye and kidney. That is why the eye spots and kidney damage go together."),

dict(img='conj', cap='Examination of the right eye',
stem="A 34-year-old man who injects heroin daily comes to the emergency department because of fever and fatigue for 6 days. He has no known heart disease. His temperature is 38.6°C (101.5°F), pulse is 106/min, and blood pressure is 122/74 mm Hg. No murmur is heard. There are no Osler nodes or Roth spots. The lower eyelid is everted as shown. Two of 2 sets of blood cultures drawn 1 hour apart grow <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i>. Rheumatoid factor is negative. Transthoracic echocardiography shows no vegetation. Transesophageal echocardiography has not yet been performed.{FIG}According to the 2023 Duke criteria, which of the following best classifies this patient's findings?",
choices=["0 major + 4 minor criteria · possible endocarditis","1 major + 1 minor · possible endocarditis","1 major + 2 minor criteria · possible endocarditis","1 major + 3 minor criteria · definite endocarditis","2 major + 2 minor criteria · definite endocarditis"], key=3,
exp="The everted lid shows <b>conjunctival hemorrhages (petechiae)</b>, a vascular minor criterion.<br><br>Count them:<br>• <b>Major (1):</b> a typical organism (<i>S. aureus</i>) in ≥2 separate blood-culture sets.<br>• <b>Minor (3):</b> predisposition (<b>injection drug use</b> counts), fever ≥38.0°C, and a vascular phenomenon (conjunctival hemorrhage).<br>• A negative transthoracic echo is <b>not</b> an imaging major criterion.<br><br>Definite endocarditis = 2 major, or <b>1 major + 3 minor</b>, or 5 minor (\"2 · 1+3 · 5\"). Possible = 1 major + 1 minor, or 3 minor. So this is <b>definite</b>. A transesophageal echo (~95% sensitive versus ~65% for transthoracic) is still indicated to look for right-sided vegetations and complications.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Under the 2023 Duke criteria, 1 major + 3 minor criteria make definite infective endocarditis. Injection drug use counts as a predisposing minor criterion, and a negative transthoracic echo does not count against the diagnosis.",
wrong={0:"This counts the positive blood cultures as a minor criterion. <i>S. aureus</i> in 2 of 2 separate sets is a <b>major</b> microbiologic criterion.",
1:"This counts only one minor criterion. Fever, injection drug use and the conjunctival hemorrhage each count separately.",
2:"This misses one minor criterion, usually injection drug use, which the 2023 criteria list as a predisposition.",
4:"This counts the echocardiogram as a second major criterion, but the transthoracic study showed <b>no</b> vegetation."},
eli5="Doctors use a checklist of big and small clues for heart-valve infection. He has one big clue (the right germ in two blood tests) and three small ones (fever, injecting drugs, eye bleeding spots). One big plus three small is enough to say 'definitely'."),

dict(img='aorticperf', cap='Aortic valve at autopsy',
stem="A 61-year-old man with poorly controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus who receives hemodialysis through a tunneled catheter is admitted because of fever, rigors, and rapidly worsening shortness of breath for 5 days. His aortic valve was normal on echocardiography 1 year ago. His temperature is 39.8°C (103.6°F), pulse is 124/min, and blood pressure is 96/38 mm Hg. A new decrescendo diastolic murmur is heard, and there are crackles throughout both lungs. Blood cultures are drawn, and antibiotic therapy is started. He dies of cardiogenic shock 36 hours later. The aortic valve at autopsy is shown.{FIG}The blood cultures most likely grew an organism with which of the following characteristics?",
choices=["Gram-negative rods, slow-growing, from the normal oral flora","Gram-positive cocci in chains, alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant","Gram-positive cocci in chains, growing in bile and 6.5% sodium chloride","Gram-positive cocci in clusters, catalase-positive, coagulase-negative","Gram-positive cocci in clusters, catalase-positive, coagulase-positive"], key=4,
exp="The aortic cusps are covered with friable vegetations, ulcerated and perforated. This is <b>acute bacterial endocarditis</b> that destroyed a <b>previously normal</b> valve within days, causing acute aortic regurgitation and cardiogenic shock.<br><br>An acute, fulminant course on a normal valve means a \"professional\" pathogen, and the commonest is <b><i>Staphylococcus aureus</i></b> (31% of endocarditis): <b>gram-positive cocci in clusters, catalase-positive, coagulase-positive</b>. Hemodialysis (~300 per 100,000) and diabetes (skin colonization, frequent punctures) are strong risk factors. It invades nearby tissue, destroys valves, and needs broad empiric therapy covering methicillin-resistant <i>S. aureus</i>, often with surgery.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Acute, destructive endocarditis of a previously normal valve is most often caused by <i>Staphylococcus aureus</i>: gram-positive cocci in clusters, catalase-positive, coagulase-positive.",
wrong={0:"Slow-growing gram-negative oral rods are the HACEK group (<i>Haemophilus</i>, <i>Aggregatibacter</i>, <i>Cardiobacterium</i>, <i>Eikenella</i>, <i>Kingella</i>). They cause indolent disease on previously damaged valves, not a 5-day destruction of a normal valve.",
1:"Alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant cocci in chains are viridans streptococci. They cause <b>subacute</b> endocarditis over weeks on a previously damaged valve, often after dental work.",
2:"Cocci in chains that grow in bile and 6.5% sodium chloride are enterococci. They cause nosocomial, usually subacute, endocarditis from urinary catheters or lines, usually on damaged valves.",
3:"Coagulase-negative staphylococci (<i>S. epidermidis</i>) infect prosthetic valves and devices through biofilm and follow a more <b>subacute</b> course. This was a native, previously normal valve destroyed in days."},
eli5="A very aggressive germ got into his blood through his dialysis line. It chewed holes in a healthy heart valve in just a few days. Only a few germs can do that, and the most common is Staph aureus."),

dict(img='candida', cap='Heart at autopsy, left ventricle and mitral valve opened',
stem="A 47-year-old woman with acute myeloid leukemia is receiving induction chemotherapy. She has been neutropenic for 3 weeks and receives total parenteral nutrition through a central venous catheter. Despite 10 days of vancomycin and cefepime, she has persistent fever. She suddenly develops a cold, pale, painful left leg, and a large embolus is removed from the femoral artery. Echocardiography shows a 2.8-cm mobile mass on the mitral valve. She dies 2 days later. Histologic examination of the embolus shows budding yeasts and pseudohyphae. The heart at autopsy is shown.{FIG}Which of the following organisms is the most likely cause of this patient's endocarditis?",
choices=["Aspergillus fumigatus","Candida albicans","Coxiella burnetii","Enterococcus faecalis","Staphylococcus epidermidis"], key=1,
exp="A huge, bulky vegetation fills the mitral orifice. <b>Fungal endocarditis</b> produces the largest vegetations, which embolize to large arteries (here the femoral) and invade the myocardium.<br><br>Fungi cause fewer than 2% of cases, almost only in <b>immunocompromised</b> patients. Her risks: neutropenia + central line + total parenteral nutrition + prolonged broad-spectrum antibiotics → <b><i>Candida</i></b> colonizes the line → fungemia → endocarditis. <b>Budding yeasts with pseudohyphae</b> identify <i>Candida</i>. Antifungals penetrate vegetations poorly, so survival is under 50% and surgery is usually needed. Therapy is caspofungin or liposomal amphotericin B, then lifelong azole suppression.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Bulky, embolizing vegetations in a neutropenic patient with a central line and parenteral nutrition suggest fungal endocarditis. Budding yeasts with pseudohyphae identify <i>Candida</i>.",
wrong={0:"<i>Aspergillus</i> also causes bulky fungal vegetations in neutropenic patients, but its tissue form is <b>narrow-angle branching septate hyphae</b>. It does not form budding yeasts.",
2:"<i>Coxiella burnetii</i> causes culture-negative, indolent endocarditis in people exposed to livestock. It has small vegetations and does not form yeasts.",
3:"<i>Enterococcus faecalis</i> is a nosocomial cause (urinary catheters, lines), but it is a gram-positive coccus in chains, not a yeast, and its vegetations are not usually this large.",
4:"<i>S. epidermidis</i> infects lines and prosthetic material through biofilm, but it is a gram-positive coccus in clusters. The embolus showed fungal elements."},
eli5="Her immune system was wiped out by chemotherapy, and a yeast got into her blood through her feeding line. It grew into a huge clump on a heart valve, and a piece broke off and blocked the artery to her leg. Under the microscope the piece looked like budding yeast, which means Candida."),

dict(img='nbte', cap='Mitral valve at autopsy',
stem="A 64-year-old man with metastatic pancreatic adenocarcinoma is admitted because of sudden left-sided weakness. MRI of the brain shows multiple small acute infarcts in different arterial territories. He has had no fever. Three sets of blood cultures, drawn before any antibiotics, show no growth. He dies 2 weeks later. At autopsy, the mitral valve is shown. Small, bland vegetations sit along the lines of leaflet closure without destruction of the underlying valve.{FIG}Which of the following best describes the composition of these vegetations?",
choices=["Bacteria enmeshed in fibrin with many neutrophils","Fibrinoid necrosis with surrounding Anitschkow cells","Immune complexes deposited on both leaflet surfaces","Loose myxoid stroma containing stellate cells","Sterile platelet–fibrin thrombi without organisms"], key=4,
exp="Small, bland vegetations along the closure lines, sterile blood cultures, multiple embolic infarcts, and advanced mucin-producing cancer point to <b>nonbacterial thrombotic endocarditis</b> (marantic endocarditis).<br><br>A hypercoagulable state (cancer, especially mucin-producing adenocarcinomas such as pancreatic cancer) + minor endothelial injury → <b>sterile platelet–fibrin thrombi</b> deposit on the valve → they break off easily → systemic emboli (strokes). The same sterile nidus is the <b>first step</b> of infective endocarditis: if transient bacteremia occurs, organisms stick to it and a septic vegetation develops.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Nonbacterial thrombotic (marantic) endocarditis consists of sterile platelet–fibrin thrombi on valves in hypercoagulable states such as cancer. The same nidus is where bacteria attach in infective endocarditis.",
wrong={0:"Bacteria enmeshed in fibrin with neutrophils is <b>infective</b> endocarditis. That would cause fever and positive blood cultures and destroy the valve.",
1:"Fibrinoid necrosis with Anitschkow cells (Aschoff bodies) is <b>acute rheumatic fever</b>, whose small verrucous vegetations follow a strep throat in a child.",
2:"Vegetations on <b>both</b> surfaces of the leaflet, in a patient with lupus or antiphospholipid antibodies, are Libman–Sacks endocarditis.",
3:"Loose myxoid stroma with stellate cells is a myxoma, a tumour (usually in the left atrium), not a vegetation along the closure line."},
eli5="His cancer made his blood extra sticky, so little sterile clots formed on a heart valve. No germs were in them. Bits broke off and blocked small arteries in his brain."),

dict(img='pet', cap='CT and fluorodeoxyglucose PET/CT of the chest (arrow and asterisk mark the region of interest)',
stem="A 71-year-old man comes to the office because of 6 weeks of low-grade fevers, fatigue, and night sweats. He had a mechanical aortic valve replacement 5 months ago. His temperature is 38.2°C (100.8°F). Three sets of blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in clusters that are catalase-positive and coagulase-negative. Transesophageal echocardiography is limited by artifact from the prosthesis. A CT and fluorodeoxyglucose PET/CT scan is shown. He receives 4 weeks of intravenous vancomycin plus rifampin. Repeat testing confirms that the isolate is still susceptible to both drugs, yet blood cultures remain positive.{FIG}Which of the following best explains the persistence of this infection?",
choices=["Bacteria surviving inside host macrophages","Efflux pumps expelling both drugs","Enzymatic inactivation of both antibiotics","Mutations altering the drug targets","Slow-growing bacteria sheltered in a biofilm"], key=4,
exp="The PET/CT shows intense glucose uptake around the prosthetic aortic valve (arrow): <b>prosthetic valve endocarditis</b>. It occurred 5 months after surgery and is caused by a <b>coagulase-negative staphylococcus</b> (<i>S. epidermidis</i>), the skin organism that rides in on devices (about 40% of prosthetic valve endocarditis).<br><br>Why it won't clear: <i>S. epidermidis</i> and <i>S. aureus</i> make <b>biofilm</b> on foreign material → a polysaccharide matrix that drugs and antibodies penetrate poorly → the bacteria inside grow slowly or stop dividing → most antibiotics need actively growing bacteria → biofilm bacteria are ~500× more tolerant, even though laboratory testing shows susceptibility. That is why therapy is long (over 6 weeks), bactericidal, rifampin-containing, and often needs removal of the prosthesis.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Prosthetic valve infection by <i>S. epidermidis</i> persists despite susceptible testing because bacteria in biofilm are poorly penetrated and slow-growing, so they tolerate antibiotics.",
wrong={0:"Intracellular survival in macrophages is the strategy of organisms such as <i>Coxiella</i>, <i>Brucella</i> and <i>Listeria</i>. <i>S. epidermidis</i> persists on the device surface, not inside host cells.",
1:"Efflux pumps are a true <b>resistance</b> mechanism and would show up as a raised minimum inhibitory concentration. The isolate remains susceptible.",
2:"Enzymatic inactivation (such as β-lactamase) is also resistance that laboratory testing detects, and vancomycin is not inactivated by staphylococcal enzymes.",
3:"Target-site mutations (such as the altered penicillin-binding protein of methicillin resistance) would appear as resistance on repeat testing. The isolate is still susceptible."},
eli5="Skin germs got onto his new artificial valve and built a slimy shield around themselves. Under the shield they sit still and hide, so medicines that only kill busy, growing germs can't reach or harm them. That is why the infection keeps coming back."),

dict(img='echoveg', cap='Transthoracic echocardiogram, apical view',
stem="A 71-year-old man comes to the office because of fatigue, low-grade fevers, and a 4-kg (9-lb) weight loss over 5 weeks. He has not had dental work or procedures, and he does not inject drugs. His temperature is 38.0°C (100.4°F). A holosystolic murmur is heard at the apex. Hemoglobin concentration is 9.4 g/dL, mean corpuscular volume is 72 μm<sup>3</sup>, and serum ferritin concentration is low. Three sets of blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in chains that grow on bile-esculin agar but not in 6.5% sodium chloride. An echocardiogram is shown.{FIG}In addition to antibiotic therapy, which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
choices=["Colonoscopy examining the whole large bowel","Dental examination with panoramic radiography","Serologic testing for Coxiella burnetii","Upper endoscopy of esophagus and stomach","Urine culture with renal ultrasonography"], key=0,
exp="The echo shows a mobile mass on the mitral leaflet (arrow), a vegetation. The organism is a <b>group D streptococcus that is not an enterococcus</b>: it hydrolyzes bile-esculin but <b>cannot grow in 6.5% sodium chloride</b>. This is <b><i>Streptococcus gallolyticus</i></b> (formerly <i>S. bovis</i>).<br><br><i>S. gallolyticus</i> bacteremia or endocarditis is strongly associated with <b>colorectal cancer</b> (and inflammatory bowel disease). The tumour breaks the mucosa and lets gut flora into the blood. Here the <b>microcytic iron-deficiency anemia</b> in an older man is another pointer to occult colonic blood loss. The lecture called this \"the answer to a board question.\"<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> <i>Streptococcus gallolyticus</i> (<i>bovis</i>) endocarditis requires colonoscopy to look for colorectal cancer.",
wrong={1:"A dental evaluation looks for the source of <b>viridans streptococci</b> or oral anaerobes. Those grow in chains but do not hydrolyze bile-esculin.",
2:"<i>Coxiella</i> serology is for <b>culture-negative</b> endocarditis with livestock exposure. His cultures are positive and identify the organism.",
3:"Upper endoscopy is reasonable for iron deficiency in general, but the organism points specifically to the <b>colon</b>, so colonoscopy comes first.",
4:"Urine culture and renal imaging look for a urinary source of <b>enterococci</b>, which do grow in 6.5% sodium chloride. This organism does not."},
eli5="This germ normally lives in the gut and gets into the blood when a colon tumour breaks the gut lining. So finding it on a heart valve is a clue to go looking for colon cancer. His low iron from slow bleeding is another clue."),

dict(img='tte', cap='Transthoracic echocardiogram, apical four-chamber view (arrows mark the lesion)',
stem="A 27-year-old man who injects heroin comes to the emergency department because of fever, cough, and right-sided pleuritic chest pain for 10 days. Yesterday he coughed up blood-streaked sputum. His temperature is 39.2°C (102.6°F), pulse is 112/min, and blood pressure is 116/70 mm Hg. There are needle track marks on both forearms. No murmur is heard. He has no history of heart disease. CT scan of the chest shows multiple peripheral nodules in both lungs, several of which are cavitating. A transthoracic echocardiogram is shown.{FIG}Which of the following best explains why this patient's infection involves this location?",
choices=["Congenital malformation of the valve leaflets","Endothelial injury from injected particles","Immune complexes depositing on the valve","Old rheumatic scarring of the valve leaflets","Turbulent high-pressure jet across the valve"], key=1,
exp="The arrows mark a vegetation in the right atrium on the tricuspid valve: <b>right-sided (tricuspid) endocarditis</b>. It is complicated by <b>septic pulmonary emboli</b> (cavitating peripheral nodules, hemoptysis, pleuritic pain). Right-sided disease often has <b>no murmur</b>.<br><br>Why the tricuspid valve in injection drug use: injected <b>particulates such as talc</b> directly damage the tricuspid and pulmonic endothelium → exposed collagen → a platelet–fibrin nidus → injected skin bacteria reach the right heart first → they colonize the nidus. <i>S. aureus</i> (skin and nasal colonization) causes 70–80% of right-sided cases. The tricuspid valve is involved in about 60% of endocarditis in people who inject drugs, versus 0–6% overall.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In injection drug use, particulate matter injures the tricuspid endothelium and skin flora reach the right heart first, so tricuspid endocarditis with septic pulmonary emboli results.",
wrong={0:"Congenital malformations (bicuspid aortic valve, ventricular septal defect) are the predisposition in children and young adults without drug use. He has no known structural disease.",
2:"Immune complexes are a <b>result</b> of endocarditis (Osler nodes, Roth spots, glomerulonephritis). They do not seed a particular valve.",
3:"Rheumatic scarring mostly affects the <b>mitral</b> valve and was the historical set-up for subacute viridans endocarditis. He has no rheumatic history.",
4:"High-pressure turbulent jets are why <b>left</b>-sided valves (mitral, aortic) are usually affected. The right heart is a low-pressure system."},
eli5="When people inject drugs, tiny gritty particles and skin germs go straight into the vein. The grit scratches the first valve it meets, on the right side of the heart. Germs then stick to the scratch."),

dict(img='aorticveg', cap='Excised aortic valve cusps',
stem="A 49-year-old man who raises sheep in Wyoming and helps with lambing each spring comes to the office because of fatigue, night sweats, and intermittent low-grade fevers for 4 months. He has a known bicuspid aortic valve. He drinks only pasteurized milk. He has not taken antibiotics in the past year. The liver and spleen are palpable. Three sets of blood cultures show no growth after 5 days. Transesophageal echocardiography shows a vegetation on the aortic valve, and he undergoes valve replacement. The excised valve is shown. Gram stain and routine culture of the valve tissue are negative.{FIG}Which of the following tests is most likely to confirm the cause of this patient's endocarditis?",
choices=["Modified acid-fast stain of the valve tissue","Periodic acid–Schiff stain of the valve tissue","Prolonged incubation of routine blood cultures","Serum IgG antibodies against phase I antigen","Urine antigen testing for Legionella species"], key=3,
exp="Vegetations on the excised aortic cusps with repeatedly sterile cultures, no prior antibiotics, and heavy exposure to sheep at lambing point to <b>culture-negative endocarditis from <i>Coxiella burnetii</i></b> (chronic Q fever).<br><br>Birthing products of sheep and goats → aerosolized <i>Coxiella</i> is inhaled → an obligate intracellular organism that will not grow on routine media → persists on an abnormal (bicuspid) valve. Diagnosis is by <b>serology (phase I immunoglobulin G titre over 1:800)</b> or polymerase chain reaction, and either is a <b>major</b> microbiologic Duke criterion. Treatment is doxycycline plus a quinolone (or hydroxychloroquine).<br><br>The commonest cause of culture-negative endocarditis overall is antibiotics given before cultures were drawn, which does not apply here.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Culture-negative endocarditis after livestock exposure suggests <i>Coxiella burnetii</i>, confirmed by phase I IgG serology (titre over 1:800) or polymerase chain reaction.",
wrong={0:"A modified acid-fast stain identifies <i>Nocardia</i>, which causes lung and brain abscesses in immunocompromised hosts, not culture-negative valve disease after livestock exposure.",
1:"A periodic acid–Schiff stain shows <i>Tropheryma whipplei</i> (Whipple disease), a rare culture-negative cause usually with diarrhea, weight loss and arthralgias. It has no link to sheep.",
2:"Prolonged incubation helps slow growers such as <i>Brucella</i> and <i>Bartonella</i>. <i>Brucella</i> comes mainly from unpasteurized dairy (he drinks only pasteurized milk), and <i>Coxiella</i> will not grow in routine cultures however long they are held.",
4:"<i>Legionella</i> urine antigen tests for Legionnaires pneumonia from contaminated water systems. It is not the test for livestock-associated endocarditis."},
eli5="Sheep birthing fluids can carry a germ that floats in the air and is breathed in. It lives inside cells and won't grow in normal lab bottles, so blood cultures stay empty. Doctors confirm it with a blood antibody test instead."),

dict(img='lyme', cap='12-lead ECG with rhythm strips',
stem="A 24-year-old woman who works as a summer camp counselor in Connecticut comes to the emergency department in August because of lightheadedness and an episode of fainting. Three weeks ago, a red patch appeared on her thigh, enlarged over a week to 12 cm, and then faded. She has had fatigue and aching joints since then. She takes no medications. Her pulse is 38/min and blood pressure is 102/64 mm Hg. Examination shows no rash or murmur. An ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?",
choices=["Borrelia burgdorferi","Corynebacterium diphtheriae","Coxsackie B virus","Rickettsia rickettsii","Trypanosoma cruzi"], key=0,
exp="The ECG shows <b>complete (third-degree) heart block</b>: P waves march regularly and the QRS complexes come at their own slow rate, with no relationship between them.<br><br>A summer in the northeastern United States + an expanding red patch that faded (erythema migrans) + fatigue and arthralgias + new conduction block a few weeks later = <b>Lyme carditis</b>. <i>Borrelia burgdorferi</i> is spread by the <i>Ixodes</i> tick. Early disseminated Lyme disease → the spirochete and inflammation involve the heart's conduction system (the atrioventricular node) → about 10% of Lyme patients develop conduction disturbances. The block usually resolves with antibiotics (ceftriaxone), sometimes with a temporary pacemaker.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> New atrioventricular block in a young person weeks after an expanding rash in a tick-endemic area is Lyme carditis (<i>Borrelia burgdorferi</i>).",
wrong={1:"Diphtheria toxin causes myocarditis with arrhythmias about a week after a grey pharyngeal pseudomembrane, not after a skin rash.",
2:"Coxsackie B virus causes myocarditis or pericarditis after a respiratory or gastrointestinal illness, with chest pain and heart failure. It does not follow an expanding rash.",
3:"<i>Rickettsia rickettsii</i> (Rocky Mountain spotted fever) causes fever, headache and a petechial rash that starts on the wrists and ankles. It is not an expanding patch followed weeks later by heart block.",
4:"<i>Trypanosoma cruzi</i> (Chagas disease) causes conduction disease decades after exposure to kissing bugs in rural Latin America, not weeks after a summer in Connecticut."},
eli5="A tick bite gave her a spreading red rash, and the germ then travelled to her heart's wiring. It blocked the signal between the upper and lower chambers. Her heart slowed right down and she fainted."),

dict(img='lympho', cap='Endomyocardial biopsy, hematoxylin and eosin stain',
stem="A 29-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of progressive shortness of breath, orthopnea, and palpitations for 1 week. Two weeks before this, he had a febrile illness with sore throat, muscle aches, and diarrhea that resolved on its own. He takes no medications or supplements and has no history of travel. His temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F) and pulse is 118/min. A third heart sound is heard. Serum troponin I concentration is increased. Coronary angiography shows normal coronary arteries. Echocardiography shows dilation of all four chambers with diffuse hypokinesis. Peripheral blood eosinophil count is normal. An endomyocardial biopsy specimen is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely cause of this patient's condition?",
choices=["Cardiac sarcoidosis","Drug hypersensitivity","Giant cell myocarditis","Helminthic infection","Viral infection"], key=4,
exp="The biopsy shows a patchy <b>lymphocytic infiltrate</b> in the interstitium with injury to nearby myocytes: <b>lymphocytic myocarditis</b>, the pattern of pathogens and mostly of <b>viruses</b>.<br><br>A viral prodrome (fever, sore throat, myalgias, diarrhea) → 1–2 weeks later new heart failure, tachycardia, an S3, a raised troponin, normal coronaries, and a globally dilated hypokinetic heart = <b>viral myocarditis</b>, peak age 20–50 years. Current adult data implicate parvovirus B19 and human herpesvirus 6. The classic board answer, Coxsackie B, shares the coxsackie–adenovirus receptor with adenovirus. Endomyocardial biopsy is definitive but invasive. Treatment is supportive: avoid exertion, and give diuretics, renin–angiotensin blockers and beta-blockers. Antivirals are unproven.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Lymphocytic myocarditis after a viral prodrome, with new heart failure and normal coronaries, is viral myocarditis. Eosinophilic infiltrates suggest hypersensitivity or parasites, granulomas suggest sarcoidosis, and giant cells suggest autoimmune giant cell myocarditis.",
wrong={0:"Sarcoidosis gives <b>non-caseating granulomas</b> in the myocardium, often with heart block and lung or node disease. There are no granulomas here.",
1:"Drug hypersensitivity myocarditis gives an <b>eosinophil</b>-rich infiltrate after a new drug (methyldopa, sulfonamides). He takes nothing, and his eosinophil count is normal.",
2:"Giant cell myocarditis is a fulminant autoimmune disease with <b>multinucleated giant cells</b> and extensive myocyte necrosis. It is not a lymphocytic infiltrate after a viral illness.",
3:"Helminths (for example trichinosis) cause <b>eosinophilic</b> myocarditis with peripheral eosinophilia and muscle pain after eating undercooked meat. His eosinophils are normal."},
eli5="A common virus he caught two weeks ago also got into his heart muscle. The body's lymphocytes came to fight it and damaged the muscle along the way. Now the heart is weak and stretched."),

dict(img='periecg', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 31-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of sharp chest pain for 2 days. The pain is worse when he breathes in or lies flat, improves when he sits up and leans forward, and radiates to the ridge of his left trapezius muscle. Ten days ago, he had an upper respiratory infection. He takes no medications. His temperature is 37.8°C (100.0°F), pulse is 96/min, and blood pressure is 124/78 mm Hg. A scratchy, high-pitched sound is heard at the left sternal border with the patient leaning forward. Serum troponin I concentration is within the reference range. Echocardiography shows a trace pericardial effusion and normal ventricular function. An ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate pharmacotherapy?",
choices=["Alteplase infusion","Aspirin and clopidogrel","Ibuprofen and colchicine","Oral prednisone","Oral valacyclovir"], key=2,
exp="The ECG shows <b>diffuse, concave ST elevation</b> across many leads with <b>PR depression</b>. With pleuritic, positional pain that radiates to the trapezius, a friction rub and a recent viral infection, this is <b>acute (viral) pericarditis</b>. Diagnosis needs ≥2 of: typical pain, rub, ECG changes, effusion. He has all four.<br><br>Treatment reduces inflammation: <b>a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug (NSAID) plus colchicine</b>. Colchicine inhibits neutrophil microtubule polymerization, speeds recovery and roughly halves recurrences. Glucocorticoids are reserved for patients who cannot take NSAIDs, because they increase recurrence. Antivirals have no proven benefit.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Acute viral pericarditis (pleuritic pain relieved by leaning forward, rub, diffuse ST elevation with PR depression) is treated with an NSAID plus colchicine.",
wrong={0:"Alteplase is thrombolysis for a <b>ST-elevation myocardial infarction</b>. That ECG shows regional ST elevation with reciprocal depression, not diffuse elevation with PR depression, and his troponin is normal. Thrombolysis in pericarditis risks hemorrhagic tamponade.",
1:"Dual antiplatelet therapy treats acute coronary syndrome. His pain is positional and pleuritic, troponin is normal, and the ECG pattern is diffuse.",
3:"Prednisone is second-line, used only when NSAIDs are contraindicated, because steroids raise the rate of recurrent pericarditis.",
4:"Antivirals have no proven efficacy in viral pericarditis, and the causative virus is usually never identified."},
eli5="A cold virus irritated the thin bag around his heart, so it rubs and hurts, especially lying down. The ECG shows the irritation across the whole heart. Anti-inflammatory pills (ibuprofen plus colchicine) calm it down."),

dict(img='waterbottle', cap='Chest x-ray, posteroanterior view',
stem="A 55-year-old woman comes to the emergency department because of progressive shortness of breath and fatigue for 3 weeks. One month ago she had a viral illness followed by sharp chest pain that improved when she leaned forward. Her pulse is 118/min, respirations are 24/min, and blood pressure is 94/68 mm Hg. The jugular veins are distended, and the heart sounds are faint. The lungs are clear to auscultation. A chest x-ray is shown.{FIG}Further examination of this patient is most likely to show which of the following?",
choices=["Early diastolic knock at the left sternal border","Inspiratory systolic pressure fall of 18 mm Hg","Inspiratory rise in jugular venous pressure","Loud third heart sound heard at the apex","Opening snap followed by a diastolic rumble"], key=1,
exp="The cardiac silhouette is enlarged and globular (\"water-bottle\" heart) with clear lungs: a <b>large pericardial effusion</b>. Hypotension, distended neck veins and faint heart sounds (<b>Beck triad</b>) mean it has progressed to <b>cardiac tamponade</b>.<br><br>Fluid in a fixed-volume sac → the chambers cannot fill → on inspiration more blood returns to the right ventricle → the septum bows into the left ventricle (the two ventricles compete for space) → left ventricular stroke volume falls → <b>systolic pressure drops more than 10 mm Hg with inspiration</b> (<b>pulsus paradoxus</b>). Emergency pericardiocentesis is needed.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> A large pericardial effusion causing tamponade gives the Beck triad and pulsus paradoxus, an inspiratory fall in systolic pressure of more than 10 mm Hg.",
wrong={0:"A pericardial knock is heard in <b>constrictive</b> pericarditis. The rigid, scarred pericardium stops filling abruptly in early diastole. It is a late complication, and the silhouette is usually normal-sized.",
2:"An inspiratory rise in jugular venous pressure (Kussmaul sign) belongs to <b>constrictive</b> pericarditis and restrictive cardiomyopathy. In tamponade, venous return still rises with inspiration, so Kussmaul sign is typically absent.",
3:"A loud S3 comes from rapid filling into a dilated, failing ventricle. In tamponade filling is <b>restricted</b>, and the lungs here are clear rather than edematous.",
4:"An opening snap with a diastolic rumble is <b>mitral stenosis</b>, which enlarges the left atrium and causes pulmonary congestion, not a globular silhouette with clear lungs."},
eli5="Fluid filled the bag around her heart and squeezed it so it can't fill properly. When she breathes in, the right side takes more room and squashes the left side. Her blood pressure dips with each breath."),

dict(img='tbperi', cap='Pericardial biopsy, hematoxylin and eosin stain',
stem="A 38-year-old man who moved to the United States from Haiti 1 year ago comes to the clinic because of fever, drenching night sweats, cough, and a 7-kg (15-lb) weight loss over 2 months. His temperature is 38.3°C (100.9°F). Heart sounds are faint, and the jugular venous pressure is elevated. HIV testing is negative. Echocardiography shows a moderate pericardial effusion with thickened pericardium. A pericardial biopsy specimen is shown. The patient declines treatment and is lost to follow-up.{FIG}This patient is at greatest risk for developing which of the following?",
choices=["Complete heart block","Constrictive pericarditis","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Mitral valve stenosis","Restrictive cardiomyopathy"], key=1,
exp="The biopsy shows granulomatous inflammation with a <b>Langhans giant cell</b> (arrow), nuclei arranged in a horseshoe. With fever, night sweats, weight loss and origin in an endemic country, this is <b>tuberculous pericarditis</b>.<br><br>Granulomatous, often caseating, inflammation of the pericardium → healing by dense fibrosis and sometimes calcification → a thick, rigid pericardial shell → <b>constrictive pericarditis</b>. The heart cannot fill in mid to late diastole, causing Kussmaul sign, a pericardial knock and right-sided congestion. The lecture noted that any pericarditis can end in constriction. Tuberculosis is the classic cause in endemic countries, and radiation and cardiac surgery are the common causes in the United States.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Tuberculous (granulomatous) pericarditis heals by fibrosis and is a classic cause of constrictive pericarditis.",
wrong={0:"Complete heart block follows inflammation of the <b>conduction system</b> (Lyme carditis, cardiac sarcoidosis, Chagas disease). This is a pericardial process.",
2:"Dilated cardiomyopathy follows injury to the <b>myocardium</b> (viral, alcohol, anthracyclines, Chagas). Tuberculosis here is confined to the pericardium.",
3:"Mitral stenosis is the late result of <b>rheumatic</b> valvulitis, which involves the valves, not the pericardium.",
4:"Restrictive cardiomyopathy is the <b>muscle</b> mimic of constriction (amyloid, radiation fibrosis). The stiffness here would come from the pericardial shell, not the myocardium."},
eli5="Tuberculosis infected the bag around his heart. As it heals, the bag turns into thick scar, like a stiff shell. The heart can't stretch to fill, which is called constrictive pericarditis."),

dict(img='myoecg', cap='12-lead ECG',
stem="A 22-year-old man comes to the emergency department because of sharp chest pain for 1 day that worsens when he lies flat and improves when he leans forward. Five days ago, he had fever, muscle aches, and a sore throat. His temperature is 37.9°C (100.2°F), pulse is 104/min, and blood pressure is 122/76 mm Hg. A pericardial friction rub is heard. Serum troponin I concentration is 5.2 ng/mL (N &lt;0.04). Echocardiography shows normal left ventricular size, normal wall motion, and an ejection fraction of 60%. Coronary CT angiography shows normal coronary arteries. An ECG is shown.{FIG}Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?",
choices=["Acute ST-elevation myocardial infarction","Dressler syndrome","Isolated acute pericarditis","Myopericarditis","Perimyocarditis"], key=3,
exp="The ECG shows diffuse ST elevation. Positional pleuritic pain, a rub and a recent viral illness establish <b>pericarditis</b>, and the markedly raised <b>troponin</b> shows the <b>myocardium is involved</b> too.<br><br>How the lecture splits the overlap:<br>• <b>Myopericarditis</b>: pericarditis predominates, with myocardial involvement but <b>normal ventricular function</b>.<br>• <b>Perimyocarditis</b>: myocarditis predominates, with <b>reduced ventricular function</b> plus pericardial features.<br>An ejection fraction of 60% with normal wall motion puts him in myopericarditis.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Pericarditis with a raised troponin but normal ventricular function is myopericarditis. If ventricular function is reduced, it is perimyocarditis.",
wrong={0:"A 22-year-old with normal coronary arteries, diffuse (not regional) ST elevation, and pain relieved by leaning forward does not have an acute coronary occlusion.",
1:"Dressler syndrome is autoimmune pericarditis weeks after a myocardial infarction or cardiac surgery. He has had neither.",
2:"Isolated pericarditis would have a <b>normal</b> troponin. His troponin is markedly increased, which shows myocardial injury.",
4:"Perimyocarditis requires <b>reduced</b> ventricular function. His ejection fraction and wall motion are normal."},
eli5="The virus irritated both the bag around his heart and a little of the heart muscle, which is why the troponin blood test is high. But the heart is still pumping normally. So the bag problem is the main one, and that is called myopericarditis."),

dict(img='palate', cap='Examination of the oral cavity',
stem="A 59-year-old man with mitral valve prolapse and moderate mitral regurgitation is brought to the emergency department by his wife because of high fever, shaking chills, and new confusion for 2 days. He has not received antibiotics. His temperature is 39.9°C (103.8°F), pulse is 126/min, respirations are 26/min, and blood pressure is 86/52 mm Hg. A holosystolic murmur is heard at the apex. Examination of the mouth is shown. Laboratory studies show a leukocyte count of 21,000/mm<sup>3</sup>. Intravenous fluids are started.{FIG}Which of the following is the most appropriate next step in management?",
choices=["Draw 3 blood culture sets, then give vancomycin and ceftriaxone","Draw 3 blood culture sets and withhold antibiotics until results","Start vancomycin and ceftriaxone first, then draw blood cultures","Obtain transesophageal echocardiography before antibiotics","Start penicillin G alone for presumed viridans streptococci"], key=0,
exp="Palatal petechiae, high fever, a predisposing valve lesion and septic shock point to <b>acute infective endocarditis</b> in an <b>unstable</b> patient.<br><br>Principles from the lecture:<br>• <b>Cultures before antibiotics</b>: bacteremia is continuous but low-grade (under 100 organisms/mL), so draw <b>3 separate sets</b> from separate sites. Antibiotics given first are the commonest cause of culture-negative endocarditis.<br>• <b>Acutely ill</b> → draw the cultures (this takes minutes) and then start empiric therapy <b>immediately</b>. <b>Stable</b> → it is reasonable to wait for culture results.<br>• Empiric therapy covers methicillin-resistant and -sensitive staphylococci, coagulase-negative staphylococci, streptococci and enterococci: <b>vancomycin + ceftriaxone (or cefepime)</b>, bactericidal.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> In suspected endocarditis with sepsis, draw 3 blood culture sets first and then immediately start empiric vancomycin plus ceftriaxone or cefepime.",
wrong={1:"Withholding antibiotics until cultures return is acceptable only in a <b>stable</b>, subacute patient. He is in septic shock.",
2:"Giving antibiotics before cultures sterilizes the blood and is the <b>leading cause of culture-negative endocarditis</b>. Drawing 3 sets takes only minutes.",
3:"Transesophageal echocardiography is important, but it must not delay antibiotics in septic shock. It can be done once treatment has started.",
4:"Penicillin G alone covers viridans streptococci but misses <i>S. aureus</i> (including methicillin-resistant strains), the commonest cause of <b>acute</b> endocarditis."},
eli5="First take blood samples to catch the germ, then start strong antibiotics right away because he is very sick. If you give antibiotics first, the germ disappears from the blood and you never find out what it was. Drawing the blood only takes a few minutes."),

dict(img='mitralveg', cap='Heart at autopsy, mitral valve opened',
stem="A 54-year-old man with mitral valve prolapse and mitral regurgitation has had low-grade fevers, night sweats, fatigue, and a 4-kg (9-lb) weight loss for 5 weeks. A molar was extracted 7 weeks ago. His temperature is 38.2°C (100.8°F). A holosystolic murmur is heard at the apex, and the spleen tip is palpable. Blood cultures grow gram-positive cocci in chains that are alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant, and bile-insoluble. Before antibiotics take effect, he develops a sudden severe headache and dies of an intracerebral hemorrhage. The mitral valve at autopsy is shown.{FIG}Which of the following bacterial products most likely allowed this organism to colonize the valve?",
choices=["Dextran","IgA protease","M protein","Pneumolysin","Protein A"], key=0,
exp="Warty vegetations line the mitral leaflets. A predisposed valve (prolapse with regurgitation), a dental extraction, a subacute course and <b>alpha-hemolytic, optochin-resistant, bile-insoluble</b> streptococci identify <b>viridans group streptococci</b> (for example <i>S. sanguinis</i> and <i>S. mutans</i>). The fatal bleed was probably a ruptured <b>mycotic aneurysm</b> from a septic embolus.<br><br>Pathogenesis: a regurgitant jet injures the endothelium → a sterile platelet–fibrin nidus forms → a dental extraction throws mouth bacteria into the blood → viridans streptococci make <b>dextran</b> (an extracellular polysaccharide made from sucrose) → dextran binds fibrin and platelets → the bacteria adhere to the nidus → a vegetation grows. Viridans streptococci cause 17% of endocarditis worldwide and less than 9% in the United States.<br><br><b>Educational objective:</b> Viridans streptococci colonize damaged valves after dental procedures because their dextran lets them stick to the platelet–fibrin nidus.",
wrong={1:"IgA protease cleaves mucosal IgA. It helps <i>S. pneumoniae</i>, <i>Neisseria</i> and <i>Haemophilus</i> colonize mucosa, not heart valves.",
2:"M protein is the antiphagocytic virulence factor of <b>group A</b> streptococci (beta-hemolytic, bacitracin-sensitive). It drives rheumatic fever by molecular mimicry, not viridans adhesion.",
3:"Pneumolysin is the pore-forming toxin of <i>S. pneumoniae</i>, which is alpha-hemolytic but <b>optochin-sensitive and bile-soluble</b>, unlike this isolate.",
4:"Protein A binds the Fc portion of IgG to block opsonization. It belongs to <i>S. aureus</i>, a gram-positive coccus in clusters, not chains."},
eli5="Pulling his tooth let mouth germs into his blood. These germs make a sticky sugar glue that grips the tiny clots on a damaged valve. Once stuck, they grew into lumps on the valve."),
]
